// [col-hashed-ordered] [kt-ordered] The canonical `cmp(Str, Str)`: a string
// compares by **code point**, not by UTF-16 code unit.
//
// `String.compareTo` on the JVM compares `char`s, i.e. UTF-16 code units,
// which puts a supplementary-plane character (encoded as a surrogate pair,
// 0xD800–0xDFFF) *below* one in the private-use area 0xE000–0xFFFF — while
// Rust's `String: Ord` is byte-wise UTF-8, which is code-point order. Salvo
// decided that a string is a sequence of characters rather than of encoding
// units (string indexing made the same call), so this is the reading that
// keeps the two backends ordering alike. Lists and tuples order through
// Salvo's own `cmp` (`core.compare`), element by element.
fun __salvoCompare(a: Any?, b: Any?): Int {
